  "content": {
    "id": "p34-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Let $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be a function satisfying $f(x+y)=f(x)+f(y)$ for all $x,y\\in\\mathbb{R}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p34-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Suppose $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$. Prove that $f(nx)=nf(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(nx)=nf(x)$.\n\nApply the additive property repeatedly to the sum of $n$ copies of $x$.",
          "worked": "For $n=1$ the result is immediate. For $n\\geq2$, repeatedly apply the given property:\n$$\\begin{align*}f(nx)&=f(x+(n-1)x)\\\\f(nx)&=f(x)+f((n-1)x)\\\\f(nx)&=f(x)+f(x+(n-2)x)\\\\f(nx)&=f(x)+f(x)+f((n-2)x)\\\\f(nx)&=\\underbrace{f(x)+\\cdots+f(x)}_{n\\text{ terms}}\\\\f(nx)&=nf(x).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 49
      },
      {
        "id": "p34-q15-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that $f(x)=cx$ for some constant $c$, whenever $x\\in\\mathbb{Q}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(x)=cx$ for every $x\\in\\mathbb{Q}$, where $c=f(1)$.\n\nUse part (a) for reciprocals of natural numbers and additivity for negative inputs.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(0)&=f(0+0)\\\\f(0)&=f(0)+f(0)\\\\f(0)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nFor any real $t$, additivity gives $f(t)+f(-t)=f(0)=0$, so $f(-t)=-f(t)$.\nFor $q\\in\\mathbb{N}$, part (a) gives\n$$\\begin{align*}f(1)&=f\\left(q\\cdot\\frac1q\\right)\\\\f(1)&=qf\\left(\\frac1q\\right),\\end{align*}$$\nso $f(\\frac{1}{q})=\\frac{f(1)}{q}$. Every rational number is $\\frac{p}{q}$ for some $p\\in\\mathbb{Z}$ and $q\\in\\mathbb{N}$. Part (a), together with the negative-input identity and $f(0)=0$, therefore gives\n$$\\begin{align*}f\\left(\\frac pq\\right)&=pf\\left(\\frac1q\\right)\\\\f\\left(\\frac pq\\right)&=\\frac pq f(1).\\end{align*}$$\nTaking $c=f(1)$ proves $f(x)=cx$ whenever $x\\in\\mathbb{Q}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 88
      }
    ]
  },
